/-!
# Quantum `SU(2)` relations and the equivariant Dirac commutator (Chakraborty–Pal)

Formalizes the algebraic core of *P. S. Chakraborty, A. Pal, "Spectral Triples and Associated
Connes-de Rham Complex for the Quantum `SU(2)` and the Quantum Sphere", Commun. Math. Phys. 240
(2003) 447–456*: the defining relations of the quantum `SU(2)` `C*`-algebra and the structure of the
`S¹×S¹`-equivariant Dirac operator.

**The quantum `SU(2)` relations (Eq. 1.2).** The algebra `A = C(SU_q(2))` is generated by `α, β` with

  `α*α + β*β = 1`,  `αα* + q²ββ* = 1`,  `αβ = qβα`,  `αβ* = qβ*α`,  `β*β = ββ*`.

From these we derive the `q`-deformed commutation relation `α*α − αα* = (q² − 1)ββ*`: the failure of
`α` to be normal is measured by `(q² − 1)` times the `β`-number operator — the deformation that
collapses to ordinary `SU(2)` (`[α*, α] = 0`) at `q = 1`.

**The equivariant Dirac operator (Eq. 2.2–2.4).** A `G = S¹×S¹`-equivariant self-adjoint operator with
discrete spectrum is diagonal on the weight basis, `D e_{ij} = d_{ij} e_{ij}` (2.2). Its commutator
with any weighted-shift generator is the *eigenvalue difference* times that shift: for the matrix-unit
transition `f_{p←q}` (weight `c`, source `q`, target `p`),

  `[D, f_{p←q}] = (d_p − d_q) f_{p←q}`.

This is the exact mechanism behind Chakraborty–Pal (2.3)–(2.4): `[D, α]e_{ij} = (d_{i-1,j} − d_{ij})
√(1−q^{2i}) e_{i-1,j}` and `[D, β]e_{ij} = (d_{i,j-1} − d_{ij}) q^i e_{i,j-1}`. The boundedness of the
commutators — and hence the summability of the spectral triple — is thus controlled entirely by the
growth of the eigenvalue differences `d_p − d_q`. The diagonal Dirac operator here is the same
diagonal-generator structure as the modular Hamiltonian `−log ρ` and the Moyal oscillator Hamiltonian:
the commutator with a matrix unit reads off the difference of its endpoint eigenvalues.

* `QuantumSU2` — the defining relations (Eq. 1.2) as a bundled structure.
* `quantumSU2_deformed_commutator` — the derived `q`-deformed relation `α*α − αα* = (q² − 1)ββ*`.
* `equivariant_dirac_commutator` — `[D, f_{p←q}] = (d_p − d_q) f_{p←q}` for the diagonal Dirac `D`.
-/

set_option autoImplicit false

open Matrix

@[expose] public section

namespace Physlib.QuantumMechanics.ComplexAction.QuantumSU2EquivariantDirac

/-- **The quantum `SU(2)` `C*`-algebra relations (Chakraborty–Pal Eq. 1.2).** A `ℂ`-star-algebra `A`
with two generators `α, β` and a deformation parameter `q` satisfying the five defining relations of
`C(SU_q(2))`. -/
structure QuantumSU2 (A : Type*) [Ring A] [StarRing A] [Algebra ℂ A] where
  /-- The quantum deformation parameter. -/
  q : ℝ
  /-- The generator `α`. -/
  α : A
  /-- The generator `β`. -/
  β : A
  /-- `α*α + β*β = 1` (the "sphere" relation). -/
  sphere : star α * α + star β * β = 1
  /-- `αα* + q²ββ* = 1` (the "co-sphere" relation). -/
  coSphere : α * star α + ((q : ℂ) ^ 2) • (β * star β) = 1
  /-- `αβ = qβα`. -/
  commuteαβ : α * β = (q : ℂ) • (β * α)
  /-- `αβ* = qβ*α`. -/
  commuteαβstar : α * star β = (q : ℂ) • (star β * α)
  /-- `β*β = ββ*` (`β` is normal). -/
  betaNormal : star β * β = β * star β

variable {A : Type*} [Ring A] [StarRing A] [Algebra ℂ A]

/-- **[Chakraborty–Pal, `q`-deformed commutator] `α*α − αα* = (q² − 1)ββ*`.** Derived from the two
relations `α*α + β*β = 1`, `αα* + q²ββ* = 1` and the normality `β*β = ββ*`: the non-normality of `α`
is exactly `(q² − 1)` times the `β`-number operator `ββ*`. At `q = 1` this vanishes and `α` becomes
normal (ordinary `SU(2)`). -/
theorem quantumSU2_deformed_commutator (S : QuantumSU2 A) :
    star S.α * S.α - S.α * star S.α = ((S.q : ℂ) ^ 2 - 1) • (S.β * star S.β) := by
  have h1 : star S.α * S.α = 1 - star S.β * S.β := eq_sub_of_add_eq S.sphere
  have h2 : S.α * star S.α = 1 - ((S.q : ℂ) ^ 2) • (S.β * star S.β) := eq_sub_of_add_eq S.coSphere
  rw [h1, h2, S.betaNormal, sub_smul, one_smul]
  abel

/-- **[Chakraborty–Pal Eq. 2.2–2.4, the equivariant Dirac commutator] `[D, f_{p←q}] = (d_p − d_q)
f_{p←q}`.** For the diagonal (`S¹×S¹`-equivariant) Dirac operator `D = diagonal d` and the matrix-unit
transition `f_{p←q} = single p q c` (which sends the source weight `q` to the target weight `p` with
amplitude `c`), the commutator is the *eigenvalue difference* `d_p − d_q` times the same transition.

Specialized to the quantum `SU(2)` generators (`p = (i-1,j)`, `q = (i,j)`, `c = √(1−q^{2i})` for `α`;
`p = (i,j-1)`, `q = (i,j)`, `c = q^i` for `β`) this is exactly Chakraborty–Pal (2.3)–(2.4). The
boundedness of `[D, ·]`, and thus the spectral triple's summability, is controlled entirely by the
growth of these eigenvalue differences. -/
theorem equivariant_dirac_commutator {D : ℕ} (d : Fin D → ℂ) (p q : Fin D) (c : ℂ) :
    Matrix.diagonal d * Matrix.single p q c - Matrix.single p q c * Matrix.diagonal d
      = (d p - d q) • Matrix.single p q c := by
  ext i j
  simp only [Matrix.sub_apply, Matrix.diagonal_mul, Matrix.mul_diagonal, single_apply,
    Matrix.smul_apply, smul_eq_mul]
  by_cases h : p = i ∧ q = j
  · rw [if_pos h, h.1, h.2]; ring
  · simp only [if_neg h]; ring

end Physlib.QuantumMechanics.ComplexAction.QuantumSU2EquivariantDirac

end

